<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Web scraping at scale isn’t a bigger version of a small scraper. It’s a different engineering problem centered on durable queues, retries, observability, and deliberate escalation. The teams that succeed treat scale as an infrastructure discipline: they design for failure, measure cost per successful page, rotate proxies with strategy rather than desperation, and climb the anti-bot ladder one rung at a time.</p>
<p>The practical upside is that you don’t have to build every layer yourself. Managed scraping APIs can absorb the hardest parts—proxy pools, browser farms, fingerprint maintenance, and retry logic—behind simple endpoints. Whether you rent infrastructure or operate it, the principles stay the same: start cheap, escalate on evidence, stay consistent, and measure everything.</p>
